@extends('layouts.app')

@section('title')Installation@endsection
@section('description')How to add Ignite to a Rocket app: the pinned Rocket, Propel and Orbit versions, DATABASE_URL and .env, the migrations, the first user and the secret key.@endsection

@section('content')
  <h1>{{ $title }}</h1>
  <p>Ignite is a Rust crate that adds an admin panel to a Rocket app. The app needs a Rust toolchain and a MariaDB or MySQL database. It does not need Node.js: the panel's built interface is part of the crate. Node.js is needed only to change that interface.</p>

  <h2>Requirements</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Rust, stable, with Cargo.</li>
    <li>MariaDB or MySQL. Ignite supports no other database.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Pinned versions</h2>
  <p>This page documents the Orbit port of Ignite, at revision <code>139d342</code> of its main branch. The port has no release tag yet, so the pins are revisions. Use the same pins as Ignite, so the app builds one copy of each crate:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Orbit (<code>orbit-orm</code>), from git, at revision <code>e3b8668</code>. Orbit is the database layer, and the models derive its <code>OrbitModel</code>.</li>
    <li>Ignite, from git, at revision <code>139d342</code>: <code>ignite-core</code> and <code>ignite-panel</code>.</li>
    <li>Rocket and Propel, from crates.io: <code>rocket</code> 0.5.2, <code>propel_rocket</code> and <code>propel_runtime</code> 0.2.0, and <code>propel_compiler</code> 0.2.0 as a build dependency. Ignite resolves these from crates.io, so the app must use the same releases. A git pin of either would build a second copy, and the panel's views would not match the app's.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Diesel is no longer used. Ignite does not need a MariaDB client library to build.</p>

  <h2>Add the crates</h2>
  <p>Add Ignite to <code>Cargo.toml</code>, with the pins above:</p>
  <pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-toml"># Ignite and Orbit, from git at the revisions above. Rocket and Propel from crates.io.
[dependencies]
ignite-panel = { git = "https://github.com/lajennylove/Ignite", rev = "139d342b27d7142b5cf37a6c6848bc5eca1b741f" }
ignite-core = { git = "https://github.com/lajennylove/Ignite", rev = "139d342b27d7142b5cf37a6c6848bc5eca1b741f" }
orbit-orm = { git = "https://github.com/lajennylove/Orbit", rev = "e3b8668270cc8c3f2c17b69b9257c442707be1a4" }
rocket = "0.5.2"
propel_rocket = "0.2.0"
propel_runtime = "0.2.0"
serde = { version = "1", features = ["derive"] }
chrono = { version = "0.4", features = ["serde"] }
dotenvy = "0.15"

[build-dependencies]
propel_compiler = "0.2.0"</code></pre>
  <p>The derive writes paths that start with <code>ignite_core</code> and <code>orbit_orm</code>, so the app needs both crates. A date field needs <code>chrono</code> with its <code>serde</code> feature. The app's CLI binary uses <code>dotenvy</code> to read <code>.env</code>.</p>

  <h2>Database URL and .env</h2>
  <p>Ignite reads the connection URL from the <code>DATABASE_URL</code> environment variable, in the form <code>mysql://user:pass@host:port/db</code>. <code>Ignite::new()</code> panics if the variable is missing. The connection is made when Rocket ignites, as an Orbit pool of 10 connections. If the database cannot be reached, the launch stops with an error.</p>
  <p>In development, put the variables in a <code>.env</code> file. <code>Ignite::new()</code> loads it automatically. A variable that is already set in the environment wins over the same name in <code>.env</code>, as in Laravel. Keep <code>DATABASE_URL</code> and <code>IGNITE_SECRET_KEY</code> only in <code>.env</code>, and keep that file out of git. Never commit a database password or a secret key.</p>

  <h2>Migrations</h2>
  <p>The panel's tables, including <code>users</code>, come from its migrations. Run them as an explicit step, before the first start and on every deploy:</p>
  <pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-bash">cargo run --bin ignite -- migrate</code></pre>
  <p>The app does not migrate when it starts. Running the migrations again does nothing. The panel's migrations are embedded in the <code>ignite-panel</code> crate, not in the app's <code>migrations</code> folder, so the app's own migration tool does not see them. Use <code>ignite migrate</code> for them.</p>
  <p><code>ignite migrate:status</code> lists each migration as Applied or Pending, and <code>ignite migrate:rollback</code> reverts the last batch: the migrations that one <code>ignite migrate</code> applied, last first, each with its <code>down.sql</code>. See <a href="/docs/deploying#rollback">Deploying</a>.</p>
  <p>The panel records its migrations in Diesel's <code>__diesel_schema_migrations</code> table. The rows that Diesel wrote before the move to Orbit are batch 0. They are the history of the panel's schema and are not run again. A rollback that reaches batch 0 reverts those migrations too, and drops the panel's tables.</p>

  <h2>Attach the panel</h2>
  <p>Attach the panel to the Rocket build with the resources the app manages. The panel's routes mount when the app starts, under <code>/admin</code>. The app's own <code>account</code> route is guarded by the panel's <code>AdminUser</code>. See <a href="/docs/panel">Panel</a>:</p>
  <pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-rust">mod article;
mod author;
mod reports;
mod views_dir;
mod migrate;
mod order;
mod mount;
mod schema;
mod tag;

use ignite_panel::Ignite;

#[rocket::launch]
fn rocket() -&gt; rocket::Rocket&lt;rocket::Build&gt; {
    rocket::build().attach(
        Ignite::new()
            .resource::&lt;article::Article&gt;()
            .resource::&lt;tag::Tag&gt;()
            .resource::&lt;author::Author&gt;()
            .csrf_exempt(&amp;["/hooks/*"])
            .mount("/admin")
            .fairing(),
    )
    .mount(
        "/",
        rocket::routes![
            reports::account,
            order::checkout,
            order::place_order,
            order::payment_hook
        ],
    )
}</code></pre>

  <h2>Create the first user</h2>
  <p>Each app has a small binary at <code>src/bin/ignite.rs</code>. It loads <code>.env</code> first, as the workspace binary does:</p>
  <pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-rust">fn main() {
    // The app reads DATABASE_URL from .env, so the CLI does too.
    let _ = dotenvy::dotenv();
    ignite_panel::cli::main();
}</code></pre>
  <p>Then create a user from the app:</p>
  <pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-bash">cargo run --bin ignite -- user create --email you@example.com --name "You"</code></pre>
  <p>The command reads <code>DATABASE_URL</code>, connects, and checks that the email is free. Only then does it ask for the password, twice, without showing it. A configuration error stops the command before the prompt. The password needs at least 8 characters.</p>
  <p><code>user create</code> does not run migrations. If the <code>users</code> table is missing, it fails and says to run <code>ignite migrate</code>, as shown in <a href="#migrations">Migrations</a>.</p>
  <p>A script can give the password instead. When standard input is not a terminal, or with <code>--password-stdin</code>, the password is the first line of standard input. The password has no environment variable.</p>
  <pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-bash">printf '%s' "$PW" | cargo run --bin ignite -- user create --email you@example.com --password-stdin</code></pre>

  <h2>Deploy and the secret key</h2>
  <p>Set <code>IGNITE_SECRET_KEY</code> before you start the app. It signs the sign-in and flash cookies, and a release build stops at startup without it. See <a href="/docs/deploying">Deploying</a> for the release build and the other steps of a deploy, and <a href="/docs/panel">Panel</a> for how to create a key.</p>
  <p>A release build renders compiled views, so the panel's views are part of the binary and a deployed app needs no view files. An app with views of its own renders them compiled in release, as the panel does. See <a href="/docs/views">Views</a>.</p>

  <h2>Developing this docs site</h2>
  <p>The steps below are for developing this documentation site, in its own repository. An app does not need them. The site needs Node.js 22.12 or newer, or 20.19 or newer, for Vite and Tailwind.</p>
  <pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-bash">npm install
npm run build   # once: writes public/build, which Rocket serves
npm run dev     # Vite, in one terminal
cargo run       # the Rocket app, in another</code></pre>
  <p>Rocket will not start until <code>public/build</code> exists, so run <code>npm run build</code> once first. In a debug build, views are read from <code>resources/views/</code> at runtime, so an edit shows on the next reload. A release build compiles them, so rebuild after an edit.</p>
@endsection
